# QuakeVault industrial appliance - stable RS-485 adapter naming.
#
# /dev/ttyUSB* renumbers across reboots and replugs, so acquisition must never
# reference it. These rules create stable aliases under /dev/quakevault-rs485-*.
#
# Two strategies, in order of preference:
#
#   1. Adapters that expose a USB serial number (FTDI FT232, most CP210x) are
#      identified by that serial. This survives being moved between ports.
#
#   2. Adapters that do not (CH340/CH341 - confirmed absent on 1a86:7523) are
#      identified by physical USB topology via KERNELS. This is stable across
#      reboots but NOT across moving the adapter to a different port: doing so
#      is a configuration change and must be recorded during commissioning.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp 99-quakevault-rs485.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=tty

# --- Strategy 1: serial-number identity (preferred) ---

# FTDI FT232 and friends.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{serial}=="?*", \
  SYMLINK+="quakevault-rs485-$attr{serial}", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="systemd"

# Silicon Labs CP210x.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{serial}=="?*", \
  SYMLINK+="quakevault-rs485-$attr{serial}", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="systemd"

# --- Strategy 2: topology identity (adapters with no serial number) ---

# CH340 on USB port 1-1. Commissioned 2026-07-31, single-sensor bench setup.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", KERNELS=="1-1", \
  SYMLINK+="quakevault-rs485-a", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="systemd"

# --- Three-sensor silo installation, commissioned 2026-08-05 ---
#
# Each sensor has its own adapter rather than sharing one multi-drop bus, so
# every adapter carries exactly one device and all three may keep the factory
# address 0x50. Address collision is a property of shared wires, and there are
# none here.
#
# That makes the physical port the sensor's only identity. These adapters report
# no serial number - confirmed on 1a86:7523 - so there is nothing else to key on.
# The aliases are named for the socket, NOT for the sensor's role: which unit is
# plugged where is configuration, recorded in acquisition.yaml and verified by
# tapping each sensor at commissioning.
#
# Moving an adapter to a different USB socket silently reassigns which sensor
# the appliance believes it is reading. On a three-sensor installation where one
# unit is the ground reference, that inverts the interpretation of everything:
# what the site did would be read as what the silo did. Treat a move as a
# configuration change and re-run the tap test.

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", KERNELS=="1-1.1", \
  SYMLINK+="quakevault-rs485-p1", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="systemd"

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", KERNELS=="1-1.2", \
  SYMLINK+="quakevault-rs485-p2", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="systemd"

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", KERNELS=="1-1.4", \
  SYMLINK+="quakevault-rs485-p4", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="systemd"
